# Introduction

The [DecontPro vignette](https://campbio.github.io/decontX/dev/articles/decontPro.html)
runs DecontPro on a sample of 500 cord blood cells, with cell-type labels
that come with the data. This article runs it on all the cells of a 10x
Genomics CITE-seq dataset, and covers two steps the vignette doesn't:

- **Labelling the cells.** DecontPro needs a cell type, or cluster, for
  each cell. Here we get them by clustering the ADTs.
- **Using the empty droplets.** 10x Genomics provides the raw matrix,
  which holds every droplet barcode, so we can give DecontPro the empty
  droplets with the `background` argument.

The data also include isotype controls, antibodies that bind no protein
on these cells, so all of their counts are contamination. They make a
useful check.

Besides decontX, the article needs these Bioconductor packages:
DropletTestFiles (the data), DropletUtils (to read them) and bluster (to
cluster), plus ggplot2 from CRAN.

```{r packages}
library(decontX)
library(DropletTestFiles)
library(DropletUtils)
library(bluster)
library(ggplot2)
```

# The data

The 10x Genomics dataset "10k PBMCs from a Healthy Donor - Gene Expression
and Cell Surface Protein" (CellRanger 3.0.0) measures 17 ADTs along with
the RNA. DropletTestFiles has both the filtered matrix, with the cells
CellRanger called, and the raw matrix:

```{r download}
getFile <- function(file) {
  getTestFile(
    paste0("DropletTestFiles/tenx-3.0.0-pbmc_10k_protein_v3/1.0.0/", file),
    prefix = FALSE
  )
}
filtered <- read10xCounts(getFile("filtered.h5"), col.names = TRUE,
                          type = "HDF5")
raw <- read10xCounts(getFile("raw.h5"), col.names = TRUE, type = "HDF5")
table(rowData(filtered)$Type)
```

DecontPro needs only the ADT counts. We keep those rows, as sparse
matrices, and shorten the ADT names:

```{r adt}
adtCounts <- function(sce) {
  isAdt <- rowData(sce)$Type == "Antibody Capture"
  x <- as(counts(sce)[isAdt, ], "dgCMatrix")
  rownames(x) <- sub("_TotalSeqB$", "", rowData(sce)$Symbol[isAdt])
  x
}
cells <- adtCounts(filtered)
droplets <- adtCounts(raw)
rownames(cells)
```

The last three ADTs are the isotype controls. One cell has no ADT counts
at all, which leaves nothing to normalize, so we drop it:

```{r zero_cells}
sum(colSums(cells) == 0)
cells <- cells[, colSums(cells) > 0]
ncol(cells)
```

The empty droplets are the droplets of the raw matrix that weren't called
as cells. We drop the ones with no ADT counts, which add nothing:

```{r empty}
empty <- droplets[, !colnames(droplets) %in% colnames(filtered)]
empty <- empty[, colSums(empty) > 0]
ncol(empty)
quantile(colSums(empty), c(0.5, 0.9, 0.99, 0.999))
```

Most empty droplets hold only a few ADT counts, but some hold hundreds.
These are probably "spongelets": droplets with no cell, but with a
fragment of membrane or other debris that soaks up antibodies. They make
the empty droplets' ADT profile differ from the cells':

```{r profiles}
round(cbind(
  cells = rowSums(cells) / sum(cells),
  emptyDroplets = rowSums(empty) / sum(empty)
) * 100, 1)
```

# Labelling the cells from their ADTs

The ADTs here mark the main blood cell types, so clustering them gives
usable labels without the RNA. We normalize each cell with the centered
log-ratio (CLR), leave out the isotype controls, and cluster with a nearest
neighbor graph:

```{r clusters}
isIsotype <- grepl("_control$", rownames(cells))
clr <- function(x) {
  x <- as.matrix(x)
  log1p(sweep(x, 2, exp(colMeans(log1p(x))), "/"))
}
normalized <- clr(cells[!isIsotype, ])

set.seed(100)
clusters <- clusterRows(
  t(normalized),
  NNGraphParam(k = 20, cluster.fun = "louvain",
               cluster.args = list(resolution = 0.5))
)
table(clusters)
```

To name the clusters, we look at the mean normalized level of each ADT in
each cluster:

```{r cluster_means}
clusterMeans <- sapply(split(seq_along(clusters), clusters), function(i) {
  rowMeans(normalized[, i, drop = FALSE])
})
round(t(clusterMeans), 1)
```

The markers give each cluster away: CD19 for B cells, CD14 for classical
monocytes, CD16 and CD56 without CD3 for NK cells, and CD3 with CD4 or
CD8a for the T cells, which CD45RA (naive) and CD45RO (memory) split
further. We write that down as rules, so the labels don't depend on the
order of the clusters:

```{r labels}
labelCluster <- function(m) {
  if (m["CD19"] > 2) return("B")
  if (m["CD14"] > 2) return("CD14 monocyte")
  if (m["CD3"] < 1 && m["CD16"] > 2 && m["CD56"] > 1.5) return("NK")
  stage <- if (m["CD45RA"] > m["CD45RO"]) "naive" else "memory"
  if (m["CD3"] > 2 && m["CD4"] > 2) return(paste("CD4 T", stage))
  if (m["CD3"] > 2 && m["CD8a"] > 2) return(paste("CD8 T", stage))
  "mixed"
}
clusterLabels <- apply(clusterMeans, 2, labelCluster)
clusterLabels
cellType <- unname(clusterLabels[as.character(clusters)])
table(cellType)
```

```{r check_labels, include = FALSE}
# Stop the knit if a refresh no longer finds the main cell types.
stopifnot(all(c("B", "CD14 monocyte", "NK") %in% cellType),
          any(grepl("^CD4 T", cellType)), any(grepl("^CD8 T", cellType)))
```

One cluster fits none of the rules. It has CD16 with some CD3 and CD4,
so it likely holds CD16 monocytes and doublets. We keep it as its own
group, `mixed`, rather than force it into a cell type.

# DecontPro without and with the empty droplets

First, DecontPro with its defaults, which estimate the ambient profile from
the cells:

```{r decontpro_default}
default <- decontPro(cells, cellType, verbose = FALSE)
```

Then with the empty droplets, which set the ambient profile instead:

```{r decontpro_background}
withEmpty <- decontPro(cells, cellType, background = empty, verbose = FALSE)
```

Each fit takes a few minutes. The share of the counts that each run
assigns to ambient and background contamination:

```{r shares}
share <- function(fit) {
  round(c(
    ambient = sum(fit$ambient_counts),
    background = sum(fit$background_counts),
    removed = sum(cells) - sum(fit$decontaminated_counts)
  ) / sum(cells), 3)
}
rbind(default = share(default), withEmpty = share(withEmpty))
```

And the share removed from each ADT:

```{r removed_by_adt}
removed <- function(fit) {
  1 - rowSums(fit$decontaminated_counts) / rowSums(cells)
}
round(cbind(default = removed(default), withEmpty = removed(withEmpty)), 2)
```

Both runs remove nearly all of the isotype controls, as they should. The
runs differ most for CD15 and CD16, which make up a larger share of the
empty droplets than of the cells, so DecontPro removes more of them when
it takes the ambient profile from the empty droplets.

## The distributions

`plotDensity()` shows the distribution of each ADT before and after
decontamination:

```{r density_default, fig.width = 10, fig.height = 6}
features <- c("CD3", "CD4", "CD8a", "CD14", "CD16", "CD19", "CD15",
              "IgG1_control")
plotDensity(cells, default$decontaminated_counts, features)
```

```{r density_background, fig.width = 10, fig.height = 6}
plotDensity(cells, withEmpty$decontaminated_counts, features)
```

`plotBoxByCluster()` shows each ADT by cell type:

```{r box_by_type, fig.width = 12, fig.height = 7}
plotBoxByCluster(
  cells, withEmpty$decontaminated_counts, cellType,
  c("CD3", "CD4", "CD8a", "CD14", "CD16", "CD19")
)
```

# When the labels are too coarse

DecontPro treats each cell type as one population: an ADT that is high in
some cells of a type and low in others looks like contamination in the low
ones. So the labels need to separate cell types whose markers differ.
Here is the same fit with one label for all T cells, which puts CD4 and CD8
T cells together:

```{r coarse}
coarseType <- sub("^CD[48] T.*", "T", cellType)
table(coarseType)
coarse <- decontPro(cells, coarseType, verbose = FALSE)
round(cbind(
  fineLabels = removed(default), coarseLabels = removed(coarse)
)[c("CD3", "CD4", "CD8a", "CD45RA", "CD45RO"), ], 2)
```

```{r density_coarse, fig.width = 10, fig.height = 6}
plotDensity(cells, coarse$decontaminated_counts,
            c("CD3", "CD4", "CD8a", "CD45RA"))
```

With one T-cell label, the high CD4 peak of the CD4 T cells is gone, and
the naive T cells' CD45RA peak shrinks: within the T cells, DecontPro
treats these real differences as contamination. If the
decontaminated counts lose a peak that the markers say is real, check
whether the labels split the cells that carry it from the cells that
don't.

# Summary

- Labels from clustering the ADTs work well when the panel marks the main
  cell types, as here. Name the clusters from their markers, and keep
  cell types whose markers differ (such as CD4 and CD8 T cells) apart.
- The empty droplets give DecontPro the ambient profile directly. In
  10x data they can include spongelets, whose profile differs from the
  cells'.
- Isotype controls, when the panel has them, show whether the
  contamination is being removed.
- The [DecontPro vignette](https://campbio.github.io/decontX/dev/articles/decontPro.html)
  covers the priors and has a FAQ.

